# Git Posture — Cursor stub

## Commit, deploy, and live vocabulary

`commit` records a repository checkpoint; it does not activate code.
`deploy` names propagation of a selected working-tree or commit identity.
`activate`, `make live`, and `go live` are synonyms for a runtime restart or
reload that is then verified. “Commit and go live” means commit, activate, and
prove the running service identity with `fleet_liveness(code_ref=...)`.
Repository timestamps and `fs(op="recent_commits")` remain a deprecated,
lower-confidence fallback during migration; they never establish validated
liveness.

**Scope:** Binds on **cursor-sdk / git-integration-worker implement substrate** and
cross-seat reasoning about shared-checkout state (uncommitted ≠ broken, no standing
git workflow). **Attended Cursor IDE** uses the live shared checkout. **cursor-sdk
generate** defaults to **Lane B** (isolated worktree) when the regime is on and scope
is in-repo; explicit `lane="A"` opts back to shared checkout.

**Lane A resident:** sole shared `master` · ¬intersecting parallel writers ·
¬`git stash` to isolate or A/B vs clean HEAD — read the tree (doctrine:
`git-posture`).

**Canonical skill (load on trigger or coding-session work):** Use the `git-posture` skill
(seat self-fetches; ¬ fs-read skill body). Entity: `agent_skill:git-posture`.
Headless `git_*` MCP (`git_commit` default; gated `git_land`/`git_integrate` only when
operator directs a merge) is covered in that skill — not a separate playbook.
